course: biology
lesson: 272
title: Mammoths and the Ice Age giants: sabre-tooths, ground sloths and woolly rhinos
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In the cartoon «Ice Age» a mammoth, a sabre-toothed cat and a ground sloth travel together, and all three were real animals, though they never talked and the sloth was far bigger than the cartoon one.
explain: Woolly mammoths were a bit smaller than an African elephant, with long hair, small ears and curly tusks: small ears and thick fur keep heat in, just as a woolly hat and earmuffs do.
explain: The frozen ground of Siberia works like a giant freezer, and in 2007 it gave up Lyuba, a baby mammoth about a month old, so well kept that there was still milk in her stomach.
explain: Ice Age people hunted mammoths and used every part; in Ukraine, at Mezhyrich, they built huts out of mammoth bones from dozens of animals.
explain: Almost everywhere people arrived, the giants vanished within a few thousand years, while the climate was also changing; elephants, rhinos and hippos in Africa are survivors of that age of giants.
fact: Woolly mammoths were a bit smaller than African elephants, with long hair, small ears and curly tusks; the last lived on Wrangel Island until about 4,000 years ago, when the pyramids of Egypt already stood.
fact: Lyuba, a baby mammoth about one month old, was found in Siberian permafrost, ground that stays frozen all year, in 2007 with her skin, trunk and even milk in her stomach kept.
fact: Ice Age people hunted mammoths and used their bones, tusks and skins; at Mezhyrich in Ukraine they built huts from the bones of dozens of mammoths.
fact: Mammoths died out as the climate warmed, their grassy steppe shrank and people hunted them; most scientists think both causes worked together, and island groups lasted longest.
fact: Smilodon, the sabre-toothed cat, had canine teeth about 18 centimetres long and a powerful neck; it was not a tiger but a cat from a separate branch.
fact: At the La Brea tar pits in Los Angeles animals got stuck in sticky tar, predators came to eat them and got stuck too, so the pits hold many more meat-eaters than plant-eaters, including thousands of Smilodon bones.
fact: Megatherium was a ground sloth as big as an elephant in South America; it stood on its hind legs to reach leaves with its long claws and died out about 10,000 years ago.
fact: Glyptodon was an armadillo relative with a shell as big as a small car, a plant-eater; early people in South America may have used empty shells as shelters.
fact: The woolly rhinoceros had thick fur and a flat front horn up to a metre long, and cave painters in France drew it more than 30,000 years ago.
fact: The cave bear was bigger than a brown bear and mostly ate plants; the giant deer called the Irish elk had antlers about 3.5 metres across, grown new each year, and was neither only Irish nor really an elk.
fact: In Australia, Diprotodon, a wombat relative as big as a hippo, and giant kangaroos lived until about 40,000 years ago and died out after people arrived.
fact: Africa kept more of its big animals, perhaps because they grew up alongside people and learned to fear them.
exercise: How big was a woolly mammoth? | A bit smaller than an African elephant | As big as a blue whale, the biggest animal | As small as a dog | Twice as tall as a giraffe
exercise: Why did mammoths have small ears and thick fur? | To keep heat in during the cold | To hear better in the deep snow | To look scary to wolves and lions | To help them float and swim
exercise: Where did the last mammoths live? | On Wrangel Island | In Egypt beside the pyramids | In the Amazon rainforest | In the Sahara
exercise: When did the last mammoths die out? | About 4,000 years ago | 66 million years ago | 100 years ago | About 1 million years ago
exercise: Who is Lyuba? | A baby mammoth found frozen in Siberia | A famous sabre-toothed cat from the tar pits | The scientist who first found the La Brea tar pits | A cave bear that lived in a zoo in Russia
exercise: Why was Lyuba so well kept? | The frozen ground worked like a giant freezer | She was kept for many years in a big jar of honey | She turned to stone the moment that she died | She was dried hard in the hot desert sun
exercise: What did people build at Mezhyrich in Ukraine? | Huts from mammoth bones | Boats from mammoth skins | Towers from mammoth tusks only | Statues of mammoths in gold
exercise: Why did mammoths die out? | A warming climate and hunting by people together | A single asteroid that hit the Earth in the Ice Age | Dinosaurs hunted them and ate them all | They all flew south for the winter and stayed
exercise: How long were the canine teeth of Smilodon? | About 18 centimetres | About 1 centimetre | About 1 metre long | About 3 metres
exercise: Was Smilodon a tiger? | No, it was a cat from a separate branch | Yes, it was a big striped tiger with fangs | Yes, it was the father of all of today's tigers | No, it was a kind of wild dog, like a wolf
exercise: Why do the La Brea tar pits hold so many meat-eaters? | Hunters came to eat stuck animals and got stuck too | Meat-eaters liked to swim in the warm tar on cold days | Plant-eaters never went anywhere near the tar pits | The tar was made from meat, so it pulled them in
exercise: What was Megatherium? | A ground sloth as big as an elephant | A giant flying bird with long sharp teeth | A huge shark from the deep sea | A woolly rhino with two horns
exercise: What was Glyptodon? | An armadillo relative with a car-sized shell | A giant tortoise that swam out from the deep sea | A sabre-toothed cat from South America | A giant snail as big as a small house
exercise: What did the woolly rhino have? | Thick fur and a long flat front horn | Feathers and a hooked beak like a bird | No horn at all, just a smooth nose | A hard shell on its back like a turtle
exercise: How wide were the antlers of the Irish elk? | About 3.5 metres | About 35 centimetres | About 35 metres | It had no antlers
exercise: What was Diprotodon? | A wombat relative as big as a hippo | A kangaroo no bigger than a rat | A giant bird that could not fly at all | A crocodile from Australian rivers
exercise: Why might Africa have kept more of its big animals? | Its animals grew up with people and learned to fear them | It was far too hot there for people to go out and hunt | People never reached Africa until a few hundred years ago | Its animals were all much too small to be worth hunting
test: «Ice Age cartoon: mammoth, sabre-tooth and sloth best mates» What is true? | All three were real Ice Age animals, but they did not make friends or talk | None of them were real; they were all made up for the cartoon film | They all really lived together with the dinosaurs, long before the Ice Age | They were all kinds of elephant, so they were family and got on well
test: «the mammoths all died with the dinos, right» What is right? | No, the last mammoths lived about 4,000 years ago | Yes, they all died 66 million years ago with T. rex | Yes, because mammoths were dinosaurs with fur | No, they are still alive in the far north of Canada
test: Count them: a cave painting shows 3 woolly mammoths and 2 woolly rhinos. How many trunks, tails and front horns in all? | 3 trunks, 5 tails and 2 front horns | 5 trunks, 5 tails and 2 front horns | 3 trunks, 3 tails and 3 front horns | 3 trunks, 5 tails and 5 front horns
test: What would happen if a deer walked into a tar pit at La Brea? | It would get stuck, and hunters coming for it might get stuck too | It would float on top of the tar and simply walk out the other side | It would turn into stone at once and stand there like a statue | The tar would wash it clean and it would run off shining
test: In the cartoon the ground sloth is small and silly. What was Megatherium really like? | As big as an elephant, standing up to reach leaves | Small enough to sit on your lap and sleep all day | A meat-eater with long sharp fangs that hunted deer | A sloth that lived in the sea and swam after fish
test: «Irish elk, so it was an Irish elk then, obviously» What is true? | It was a giant deer, not an elk and not only Irish | Yes, a true elk that lived only in Ireland and nowhere else | It was a kind of moose with no antlers | It was a kind of horse
test: Why could scientists find milk inside baby Lyuba after thousands of years? | Permafrost froze her quickly and kept her like a freezer | Milk never goes bad, however long it is kept | Someone found her and fed her milk later on | She was found inside an old fridge in a hut
test: «mammoth bone houses?? no way» Is that real? | Yes, Ice Age people in Ukraine built huts from mammoth bones | No, it is only in cartoons and films, never in real life | Yes, but they were really dinosaur bones, not mammoth bones | No, people at that time lived only in trees
test: A huge armadillo shell is found in Argentina. Who might have used it as a shelter? | Early people in South America | The very last dinosaurs | Sabre-toothed cats | Nobody could ever have
test: «where did all the giant animals go» What is the best short answer? | Many vanished after people arrived, as the climate also changed | They are all still hiding in the deepest forests, waiting | They all turned into small animals overnight | They all left the land on a big ship long ago
test: Which big animals of today are survivors of the age of giants? | Elephants, rhinos and hippos | Cats, dogs and rabbits | Sparrows, pigeons and crows | Goldfish and frogs
exam: Identify the principal adaptations of the woolly mammoth to a cold climate. | Small ears and a dense coat of hair, which retained bodily heat | Large ears and bare skin, which shed heat to prevent overheating | A shell of bony plates which kept out the wind and the snow | Webbed feet for swimming beneath the ice of frozen lakes
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the extinction of the woolly mammoth. | It is generally attributed to a warming climate in combination with human hunting | It is attributed to the impact of an asteroid at the close of the last glacial period | It is attributed to predation by the last surviving dinosaurs of the northern regions | The species is understood to survive at the present day in the remote north
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the La Brea tar pits contain a disproportionate number of carnivores. | Predators attracted to trapped animals became trapped in turn | Herbivores were incapable of reaching the site owing to the surrounding cliffs | The tar was preferred by carnivores as drinking water | Herbivore bones dissolve completely in tar
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the animal popularly termed the Irish elk? | It was a giant deer bearing antlers of some three and a half metres, found well beyond Ireland | It was a true elk confined entirely to Ireland, where it was hunted to extinction by farmers | It was a horse of considerable size, so named for the shape of its long head | It was an elk lacking antlers, distinguished only by its great height
